Static Testing in Software Development
1. Definition & Purpose
Static testing = examining code, requirements, or design without executing the program.

Goal: detect defects early → reduce cost, improve quality.

Examples: reviews, walkthroughs, inspections, static analysis tools.

2. Code Static Analysis
Checks performed by tools:

Coding standard violations → e.g., naming conventions not followed.

Security vulnerabilities → e.g., SQL injection risks.

Unreachable code → dead code blocks.

Variables used without initialization → runtime errors avoided.

Memory leaks → improper resource handling.

Poor code structure/complexity → maintainability issues.

Nature: Automated, tool-based, no execution required.

3. Characteristics of Static Testing
Can be applied to non-executable artifacts (requirements, design docs).

Reveals potential issues that could become defects.

Identifies defects directly without execution.

MCQ Example: Correct combination → A, C, D.

4. Types of Static Reviews
Informal Review: Quick, unstructured, e.g., peer read-through.

Walkthrough: Author explains work product to stakeholders for feedback.

Technical Review: Structured, experts check against standards/specs.

Inspection: Most formal, defined roles (moderator, reader, recorder, inspector), entry/exit criteria, defect-focused.

Example: Financial services blueprint → Walkthrough is most appropriate.

5. Formal Review Process
Planning: Define scope, participants, roles, entry/exit criteria.

Initiation: Distribute materials, explain objectives.

Individual Review: Participants examine independently, prepare issues/questions.

Issue Communication & Analysis: Meeting to discuss/document findings.

Fixing & Reporting: Assign fixes, track progress, report outcomes.

MCQ Example: Correct statement → B. ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-3 describes review techniques.

6. Review Roles
Author: Creates work product.

Moderator/Facilitator: Leads process, mediates.

Reviewer: Examines product, identifies issues.

Scribe/Recorder: Documents findings and decisions.

Manager: Provides resources, ensures objectives met.

MCQ Example: Manager’s responsibility → B. Ensuring sufficient time available.

7. Success Factors for Reviews
Clear objectives.

Right review type chosen.

Adequate preparation time.

Management support.

Proper issue tracking & metrics collection.

Training for participants.

Culture of learning & improvement.

8. Static vs Dynamic Testing
Static Testing	 | Dynamic Testing
No execution	| Requires execution
Applies to docs/code	| Applies to executable code
Finds defects directly	| Shows failures caused by defects
Early lifecycle	Runtime
Highlights structure/style issues	Shows actual behavior
Examples: reviews, inspections	Examples: functional, performance testing
